  "worked_solution": "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector. To find the velocity vector, we first need to integrate the acceleration vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \u27e8-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},0\u27e9\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\u27e8\\int (-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})\\text{d}t,\\int 0\\,\\text{d}t\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{1}{t}+{C}_{1},{C}_{2}\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(1)=\u27e81,-1\u27e9.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =\u27e8\\frac{1}{1}+{C}_{1},{C}_{2}\u27e9 \\\\ \u27e81,-1\u27e9 & =\u27e81+{C}_{1},{C}_{2}\u27e9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=-1.$ Therefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\u27e8\\frac{1}{t},-1\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the position vector by integrating the velocity vector.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \u27e8\\frac{1}{t},-1\u27e9\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\u27e8\\int \\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (-1)\\,\\text{d}t\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\operatorname{ln}\u2061t+{C}_{3},-t+{C}_{4}\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(1)=\u27e82,3\u27e9.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =\u27e8\\operatorname{ln}\u20611+{C}_{3},-1+{C}_{4}\u27e9 \\\\ \u27e82,3\u27e9 & =\u27e8{C}_{3},-1+{C}_{4}\u27e9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{3}=2$ and ${C}_{4}=4.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\u27e82+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061t,4-t\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
